 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. In September 2026, landlords paid **0.58 percentage points more** than owner-occupiers on a standard variable bank loan.\n\n**Standard variable rates, September 2026 (RBA table F5)**\n- Investor (landlord): **9.35%**\n- Owner-occupier: **8.77%**\n- Gap: **0.58 percentage points**\n\n**How this has changed in the past year**\n- The gap has stayed at 0.58 points since September 2025. Back then, the rates were 8.60% for landlords and 8.02% for owner-occupiers.\n- Both rates went up by the same amount, 0.75 points in total, in three 0.25-point steps: February, March and May 2026.\n\n**Discounted rates have a smaller gap**\n- Landlords: 7.13%\n- Owner-occupiers: 6.80%\n- Gap: 0.33 percentage points (September 2026)\n\nThese are the banks' advertised rates. Most borrowers actually pay less than the standard variable rate, closer to the discounted rate or below it. The standard rate shows how lenders price the two types of loan, but it isn't what a typical borrower pays.",
